From 377fe326eab9a7fa9f18bc3b28ce5fe73b32a13f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Tue, 1 Sep 2026 07:52:26 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Lay a noise floor under lip-sync audio instead of digital
 silence
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

On a generated-character render the model's behaviour through the gaps
between sentences was degenerate: rather than closing the lips it held
the mouth half-open with a black void where the teeth should be and a
lower lip that lost all form, while articulating cleanly the moment
speech resumed. Four of that clip's seven pauses measured above its own
mean mouth aperture. Timing was right; the rest state was wrong.

Those gaps come from silence(), which is Buffer.alloc — every sample
exactly zero. True digital silence does not occur in the recordings
these models are trained on. The floor puts the model's input back
inside the distribution it has actually seen: uniform noise at a peak
of -60 dBFS, roughly 40dB under ordinary speech.

Scoped to sliceNarration, whose only caller is the lip-sync path. The
narration track the viewer hears is untouched — worth being careful
about, because the compositor runs loudnorm over that track twice and
a floor added there could be amplified into audible hiss.

Applied uniformly rather than only to detected silence: at this level
it changes no speech feature the model reads, and detecting silence to
treat it specially would add a failure mode for no gain. Deterministic
by construction (a small LCG, not Math.random), so a retry renders the
same bytes and a test cannot flake. Non-16-bit audio is returned
untouched rather than corrupted.

sliceNarration now copies before writing rather than handing back a
view into the caller's PCM; writing a floor into a view would have
corrupted the track the video is composed from. A test pins that.

This is a HYPOTHESIS with one supporting observation, not a proven fix,
and it is deliberately cheap to undo: delete one call in sliceNarration
and the model receives exactly what it received before. Two existing
tests asserted bit-exact sample values and now assert provenance within
tolerance, which is the intended change rather than a workaround.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
